Error: case_when() Missing TRUE


Problem


Show the code
bp_clean |&gt; 
  mutate(
    age_cat = case_when(
      age &lt; 40 ~ "Young",
      age &gt;= 60 ~ "Senior"
    )
  ) |&gt; 
  select(age, age_cat)


#&gt; # A tibble: 200 × 2
#&gt;      age age_cat
#&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;chr&gt;  
#&gt;  1    36 Young  
#&gt;  2    79 Senior 
#&gt;  3    19 Young  
#&gt;  4    70 Senior 
#&gt;  5    61 Senior 
#&gt;  6    43 &lt;NA&gt;   
#&gt;  7    49 &lt;NA&gt;   
#&gt;  8    23 Young  
#&gt;  9    49 &lt;NA&gt;   
#&gt; 10    72 Senior 
#&gt; # ℹ 190 more rows



Solution


Show the code
bp_clean |&gt; 
  mutate(
    age_cat = case_when(
      age &lt; 40 ~ "Young",
      age &gt;= 60 ~ "Senior",
      TRUE ~ "Middle"
    )
  )

What is a confounder?





%%{init: {'flowchart': {'nodeSpacing': 40, 'rankSpacing': 70, 'padding': 14, 'htmlLabels': false}, 'themeVariables': {'fontSize': '22px', 'fontFamily': 'Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif'}}}%%
flowchart LR
  A["Age / BMI&lt;br/&gt;(confounders)"] --&gt; S["Salt intake&lt;br/&gt;(exposure)"]
  A --&gt; B["Systolic BP&lt;br/&gt;(outcome)"]
  S ==&gt;|"the effect we want"| B
  classDef conf fill:#fdebd0,stroke:#e67e22,stroke-width:2px;
  classDef exp fill:#d6eaf8,stroke:#3498db,stroke-width:2px;
  classDef out fill:#fadbd8,stroke:#e74c3c,stroke-width:2px;
  class A conf; class S exp; class B out;







A confounder is associated with the exposure and the outcome, and is not on the causal path between them. Ignoring it mixes its effect into the crude estimate.

Table 1 with tbl_summary()

library(gtsummary)
ncd |&gt;
  select(age, sex, residence, education, bmi, salt_g, tobacco, alcohol, phys_act, htn_f) |&gt;
  tbl_summary(by = htn_f, missing_text = "(Missing)") |&gt;
  add_overall() |&gt; add_p()



Point at the Missing rows, the medians for continuous variables and the p-values. Table 1 compares groups one variable at a time: it cannot adjust, and it cannot tell us about causation.

Functions and Control Flow

Functions are reusable blocks of code created with function()
Control flow uses if, else, for, while and repeat



Show the code
add &lt;- function(a, b) {
  result &lt;- a + b
  return(result)
}

if (add(2, 3) &gt; 4) {
  print("Yes, it is greater than 4")
}



Functions help organize logic and avoid repetition
Control flow enables conditional decisions and loops
